Jira Link: [DB-545](https://yugabyte.atlassian.net/browse/DB-545)
See also:

[Bizarre behavior on attempt to handle 23505 error in PL/pgSQL](https://github.com/yugabyte/yugabyte-db/issues/6429)

and: [Bad behavior in PL/pgSQL block statement with exception section on 'unique_violation' (code 23505)](https://github.com/yugabyte/yugabyte-db/issues/12184).

_Using YB-2.4.0.0 single-node cluster on macOS._

First, here's a positive test:

```
drop table if exists t;
create table t(k varchar(3) primary key);
insert into t(k) values('dog');

do $body$
begin
  insert into t(k) values('frog');
exception when others then
  raise info 'exception caught';
end;
$body$;
```

The block happily finishes with "exception caught". Here's a seconf postive test:

```
do $body$
begin
  raise unique_violation;
exception when others then
  raise info 'exception caught';
end;
$body$;
```

Here, too, it happily finishes with "exception caught". Now to the bug. With the table left as is, do this:

```
do $body$
begin
  insert into t(k) values('dog');
exception when others then
  raise info 'exception caught';
end;
$body$;
```


Now the exception _is NOT caught_. Rather, the block finishes with this error:

```
duplicate key value violates unique constraint "t_pkey"
```

Repeat the test in Vanilla PG. (I have only Version 13.2 so I cannot try the 11.2 that YSQL uses.) Here, the third block (of course!) also catches the exception as one has been taught to expect.
